{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>If you afflict him in any way</b> — this is an elliptical verse; it is truncated and does not specify his punishment, like \"therefore whoever kills Cain\" (Genesis 4:15), where the punishment is not specified. So too here, אִם עַנֵּה תְעַנֶּה אֹתוֹ is an expression of truncation: you will ultimately receive your due. Why? כִּי יִצְעַק אֵלַי — I will hear his cry and take vengeance. This is the language of Rashi. But it is not correct, and even the proof he brought does not testify to it.\n\nRather, it is possible that כִּי in this instance means אִם (\"if\"), which is one of its usages. He would be saying: if he cries out to me, I will surely hear his cry. And the doubling is for emphasis and reinforcement of the matter, in the manner of הַמִּבְּלִי אֵין קְבָרִים (Exodus 14:11), הֲרַק אַךְ בְּמֹשֶׁה (Numbers 12:2).\n\nBut the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he says: if you afflict him, let him but cry out to me alone — immediately I will hear his cry. He needs nothing else at all, for I will save him and avenge him against you. The reason: you oppress him because he has no savior from your hand, yet behold he is more helped than any man, for other people must trouble themselves to find deliverers to save them and helpers to avenge them, and perhaps they will not avail and the rescue will not succeed — but this one, by his cry alone, is saved through HaShem and avenged against you, for HaShem is a vengeful Elohim and master of wrath (Nahum 1:2). And this concept appears in many verses, such as what is said: \"Do not rob the poor because he is poor, and do not crush the afflicted in the gate, for HaShem will plead their cause\" (Proverbs 22:22–23) — he says, do not rob the poor because he is poor and has no helpers, and do not crush the afflicted who is in your gates, for HaShem will plead on their behalf. And similarly it says: \"Do not enter the field of the fatherless, for their redeemer is strong; YHWH of Hosts is His name\" (Proverbs 23:10–11) — they have a redeemer who is stronger and closer than any man. So too here he says that by his cry alone he will be saved.\n\nAnd like it: \"For as the rain and the snow come down from heaven and do not return there except they water the earth and make it bring forth and sprout and give seed to the sower and bread to the eater, so shall My word be that goes out from My mouth; it shall not return to Me empty except it accomplish what I desire\" (Isaiah 55:10–11). In both of them he says that they will do nothing else except water the earth immediately, and likewise except accomplish what I desire — and behold it has the sense of אֶלָּא (\"but rather\"), and similarly: כִּי אִם אֶל אַרְצִי וְאֶל מוֹלַדְתִּי אֵלֵךְ (Numbers 10:30)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "Ramban initially paraphrases Rashi's reading of the verse as elliptical; when he disputes it, the word 'כי' (for/because) becomes the crux — whether it introduces the reason for an implied punishment or means 'if.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:14:31.353620642Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}